= Analysis of TCP and UDP Protocols

== Asynchronous vs. Synchronous Communication

TCP is a *synchronous* protocol, whereas UDP is *asynchronous*.

- *TCP (Synchronous):* It has a connection-oriented model. Before tranmission, a three-way handshake is mandatory. The entire data transfer happens within the context of this synchronized session.

- *UDP (Asynchronous):* It has a connectionless fire-and-forget model. A process can send a UDP datagram at any time without any handshake with the receiver.

*NOTE*: For a single `send()` primitive, both protocols behave asynchronously, as the call returns to the application without waiting for the remote host to `recv()` it.

== Blocking vs. Non-blocking Communication

This is a configurable behavior of the socket API, not the protocols themselves. An application can configure a socket for either TCP or UDP to operate in either of the two modes:

- *Blocking (Default):* A blocking call will suspend the execution of the application process until the operation is complete. For a `send()`, this means waiting until the data has been copied from the user buffer to the kernel buffer. For a `recv()`, this means waiting until the data arrived in the kernel buffer has been copied into the user buffer. 

- *Non-blocking:* A non-blocking API call returns control to the process immediately, regardless of whether the operation could be completed.

